  "worked_solution": "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sqrt{x})y=x\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $Q(x)=x\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
